-- C++ binding for anthill.prelude.Float. Proposal 038 — host-specific spec
-- satisfaction lives separately from the language-agnostic spec in
-- stdlib/anthill/prelude/float.anthill.
--
-- WI-886: the C++ peer of `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill`, and the file
-- that replaced cpp-gen's hand-written `Float` tables.
--
-- `host_fn` here is a C++ EXPRESSION TEMPLATE — `$1`, `$2`, … stand for the operation's
-- arguments. `docs/kernel-language.md` §10.2 defines that reading and why it is not a
-- bare function name; cpp-gen checks each template's slots against the operation's
-- declared arity (`HostOpTable::from_kb`).
--
-- The `#include` a spelling needs is NOT here: it rides the `IncludeMapping` probe
-- table in `stdlib/anthill/realization/cpp_std.anthill`, which is where "host spelling
-- -> header" already lives for cpp. A mapping whose spelling is not probed there emits
-- a header that does not compile, which
-- `every_std_spelling_a_cpp_mapping_emits_has_an_include_probe` is what prevents.
--
-- NO `carrier` CLAUSE, and that is load-bearing rather than an omission: `Float`'s
-- C++ spelling is `TypeMapping(anthill_type: "Float", host_type: "double")` in
-- `cpp_std.anthill`, which is the PROFILE-OVERLAYABLE channel — a `cpp20-stl` overlay
-- can remap it. `CarrierTable` is consulted AHEAD of that query, so adding
-- `carrier { Float: "double" }` here silently disables the overlay. MEASURED:
-- `keyed_overlay_test::profile_threads_into_declared_signature_lowering` fails with the
-- clause present. (`int64.anthill` beside this one still carries a `carrier` clause and
-- therefore still shadows an `Int64` overlay — pre-existing, and no test pins it.)
namespace anthill.prelude
  provides Float language cpp
    -- IEEE comparison. `std::isgreater` and friends are `<cmath>`'s ordered
    -- comparisons: false when either operand is NaN, which is exactly what
    -- `Float`'s `PartialOrd` means and what the rust `float_gt` family answers.
    -- (Bare `>` would answer the same for IEEE doubles; the named forms are used
    -- because they also suppress the FP exception a quiet-NaN comparison raises.)
    --
    -- `max`/`min` are IEEE-754 `maxNum`/`minNum` — `std::fmax`/`std::fmin`, NOT
    -- `std::max`/`std::min`. The `f` versions ABSORB NaN, which is what
    -- `Float.max`/`min` mean (WI-881) and what the interpreter's `f64::max`/`min`
    -- do; the comparison-based `std::max` answers differently on a NaN operand.
    --
    -- `floor`/`ceil`/`round` RETURN `Int64` in anthill and `double` in `<cmath>`,
    -- so each carries the cast. Without it the value still converted — a `return`
    -- statement narrows implicitly — but silently, and only because the enclosing
    -- signature happened to say `int64_t`; written here it is the mapping's own
    -- statement of what it produces. (They are PARTIAL: `floor(nan)` and
    -- `floor(1e300)` have no `Int64` answer. The rust host raises; C++ narrowing is
    -- undefined for an out-of-range double. Giving them a guarded `Error` row, which
    -- is what would say so in the SIGNATURE, is WI-882's shape — the two hosts will
    -- diverge until then.)
    --
    -- `recip` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT, as it is from the rust binding: its equation in
    -- `stdlib/anthill/prelude/float.anthill` carries `[simp]`, so `recip(a)` is
    -- inlined to `div(1.0, a)` at LOAD and never reaches any backend. Every other
    -- declared operation of the sort is here.
    operation_map {
      gt: "std::isgreater($1, $2)",
      gte: "std::isgreaterequal($1, $2)",
      lt: "std::isless($1, $2)",
      lte: "std::islessequal($1, $2)",
      max: "std::fmax($1, $2)",
      min: "std::fmin($1, $2)",

      abs: "std::fabs($1)",
      neg: "(-$1)",
      div: "($1 / $2)",
      floor: "static_cast<int64_t>(std::floor($1))",
      ceil: "static_cast<int64_t>(std::ceil($1))",
      round: "static_cast<int64_t>(std::round($1))",

      isNaN: "std::isnan($1)",
      isInfinite: "std::isinf($1)",
      isFinite: "std::isfinite($1)",

      sqrt: "std::sqrt($1)",
      hypot: "std::hypot($1, $2)",
      fmod: "std::fmod($1, $2)",
      pow: "std::pow($1, $2)",

      sin: "std::sin($1)",
      cos: "std::cos($1)",
      tan: "std::tan($1)",
      asin: "std::asin($1)",
      acos: "std::acos($1)",
      atan: "std::atan($1)",
      atan2: "std::atan2($1, $2)",

      exp: "std::exp($1)",
      log: "std::log($1)",
      log10: "std::log10($1)",
      log2: "std::log2($1)",

      -- Inline literals rather than `std::numbers::pi_v<double>`: those are C++20
      -- and cpp-gen targets C++17, and a literal needs no header at all.
      pi: "3.141592653589793",
      e: "2.718281828459045",
      tau: "6.283185307179586"
    }
  end
end

-- NO SPEC-SATISFACTION FACTS (`fact PartialEq[T = Float]`, `fact Numeric[T = Float]`,
-- …), unlike the rust binding and unlike `int64.anthill` beside this file. MEASURED:
-- the whole cpp-gen suite is green without them, so nothing codegen does needs `Float`
-- to dispatch a typeclass operation. `int64.anthill` keeps its set because the harness
-- says the typer's primitive dispatch in these tests does need THOSE (see
-- `tests/common/mod.rs`, `cpp_bindings_dir`). A `provides` fact is not free — it enters
-- coherence and dispatch — so this file asserts only what it is for: the C++ spellings.
